# Gateway aggregate live reads

Candidate 26.09.1; read-only behavior. This document is not release approval.

The following GET routes preserve the existing gateway, organization and site
admission checks, then validate the normalized adapter result:

- /firewall/gateways/{id}/vpn
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/device-summary
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/tailscale
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/haproxy
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/haproxy/servers
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/haproxy/backends
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/haproxy/frontends
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/proxy
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/crowdsec
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/telegraf
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/monit
- /firewall/gateways/{id}/netflow

Unsupported adapter results return 501. Failed connections, failed required
reads and malformed normalized results return 502 with a fixed public message.
Scoped or absent gateways retain their existing opaque 404 behavior. Adapter
metadata cannot replace the admitted gateway identity. Cancellation propagates.

The OPNsense aggregates require every component read to complete successfully.
A failed request cannot silently become an empty component. This is a transport
and normalized-envelope guarantee: the underlying endpoint parsers still need
firmware-specific validation of successful responses. In particular, a malformed
HTTP 200 body can still be interpreted as defaults by some underlying parsers.
Do not treat this change as proof that every upstream response schema is correct.

Normalized inventory lists require consistent counts and scalar row fields.
Service flags must be booleans. The OPNsense summary requires balanced interface
counts; the separate existing OpenWrt summary retains its own explicit contract.
The admitted vendor selects the summary contract, not vendor fields from an
adapter result. Known sensitive keys are redacted before API delivery.

The selected dashboard panels distinguish loading and unavailable reads from
confirmed data. They hide cached values while refreshing and after failure,
provide an explicit retry, and reject a response for another gateway. Monit,
Telegraf, NetFlow and CrowdSec use the normalized API fields. VPN raw-status
rendering additionally masks known secret fields regardless of value shape and
masks any container beyond its recursion limit. Key-based redaction does not
promise to identify arbitrary secret text under an unknown field name.

A configuration flag is not proof of functioning traffic forwarding, monitoring,
VPN connectivity or security enforcement. CrowdSec's existing bounded result
window is not a complete incident history. Device-summary updates do not qualify
other independent overview queries, firmware status or all monitoring metrics.

Qualification uses actual adapter parsers, HTTP client fault injection, isolated
PostgreSQL/ASGI requests and the actual dashboard. Vendor I/O and focused test
identities are synthetic; real image/firmware acceptance remains required.
There are no device writes, service restarts, firewall changes, plugin installs,
restore operations or production traffic tests in this correction.

Before deployment, rehearse the supported image on the exact intended firmware
and installed plugins. Confirm failure, recovery and operator-visible states on
an isolated target. Follow docker/RELEASE-SUPPORT for support and release gates;
this change does not establish HA, recovery objectives or enterprise readiness.
